[{"id":47289,"sender_id":335,"sender_full_name":"bezem","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/976fe996b6de88bb8bc6aeecf4906d78?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47289\">#1</a> Why is a geometric theory on http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/geometric+theory defined as a _first-order_ theory with _possibly infinitary_ disjunction? The latter would render compactness false, and compactness is one main characteristic of first-order logic (see http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lindstr%C3%B6m%27s_theorem). I think this definition is self-contradicting, or in any case not in accordance with (classical) abstract model theory.</p>\n<p>Marc</p>","timestamp":1403111614,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47290,"sender_id":138,"sender_full_name":"Zhen Lin","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/5284233bbaf108722eea4bbf89fdeda2?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47290\">#2</a> It is “first order” in the sense of not quantifying over subsets/predicates/relations. Nothing more than that is meant.</p>","timestamp":1403112082,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47292,"sender_id":29,"sender_full_name":"Todd_Trimble","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/078428beaeaddf058781b9be6b2c240f?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47292\">#3</a> It’s a very standard  and well-established notion. See Johnstone’s “Elephant” book on topos theory for more information.</p>","timestamp":1403115779,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47293,"sender_id":204,"sender_full_name":"IngoBlechschmidt","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/eb9f7e57a3c762c6d98139a2a349fae9?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47293\">#4</a> Also note that the class of geometric formulas is extremely interesting for reasons concerning base change: The truth of geometric formulas is preserved under pullback by geometric morphisms; this is detailed in the <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/geometric%20theory\">article</a> you linked to.</p>\n<p>If you don’t care about topos theory, here is a more explicit way to state a related result. Recall that any modal operator <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Box</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.675em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span></span></span></span> gives rise to a translation <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>↦</mo><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi \\mapsto \\varphi^\\Box</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7054em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">↦</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0299em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8355em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord amsrm mtight\">□</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> of formulas. For instance, the well-known modal operator <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Box = \\neg\\neg</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.675em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">¬¬</span></span></span></span> gives rise to the <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/double-negation%20translation\">double-negation translation</a>, and the operator <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⇒</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Box\\varphi = ((\\varphi \\Rightarrow F) \\Rightarrow F)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8694em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">((</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇒</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇒</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> gives rise to Friedman’s <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span>-translation. (Here, a <em>model operator</em> should satisfy the following axioms: <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi \\Rightarrow \\Box\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇒</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8694em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span></span></span></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><mi>φ</mi><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Box\\Box\\varphi \\Rightarrow \\Box\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8694em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□□</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇒</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8694em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span></span></span></span>, and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>⇔</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><mi>φ</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Box(\\varphi \\wedge \\psi) \\Leftrightarrow \\Box\\varphi \\wedge \\Box\\psi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">∧</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">ψ</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇔</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8694em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">∧</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">ψ</span></span></span></span>. This is, in a somewhat different language, detailed at <em><a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney%20topology\">Lawvere-Tierney topology</a></em>.)</p>\n<p>The result is the following: If <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span></span></span></span> is a geometric formula, then <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>φ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi></msup><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi^\\Box \\Rightarrow \\Box\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0299em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8355em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord amsrm mtight\">□</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⇒</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8694em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span></span></span></span>, i.e. all <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Box</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.675em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span></span></span></span>’s appearing in the <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Box</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.675em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span></span></span></span>-translation may be pulled to the front.</p>","timestamp":1403116850,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47294,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47294\">#5</a> If this terminology, standard and well-established in topos theory and related schools of thought, is not recognized in some parts of the community such as, I gather from <a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47289\">#1</a>, classical abstract model theory, then it would still be good to have a warning remark in the entry saying just this, for clarification. Something like </p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Remark: While this terminology is standard in … beware that there is another convention in …</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Might any of the experts here have the energy to briefly add something like this to entry? That would be nice.</p>","timestamp":1403117329,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47297,"sender_id":335,"sender_full_name":"bezem","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/976fe996b6de88bb8bc6aeecf4906d78?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47297\">#6</a> My point is not about different terminology in different communities: click on http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Elephant, scroll to D1.1, see under theories, and there are two subcategories: first-order and infinitary first-order. The way I understand it: \"geometric\" should use the latter (infinitary only for disjunction), \"coherent\" the former.</p>","timestamp":1403120242,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47298,"sender_id":335,"sender_full_name":"bezem","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/976fe996b6de88bb8bc6aeecf4906d78?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47298\">#7</a> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47293\">#4</a>: Is the result still true for infinitary disjunctions? (I mean, pulling boxes to the front)</p>","timestamp":1403120702,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47300,"sender_id":138,"sender_full_name":"Zhen Lin","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/5284233bbaf108722eea4bbf89fdeda2?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47300\">#8</a> What exactly is your objection? The <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/red%20herring%20principle\">red herring principle</a> perhaps?</p>","timestamp":1403125031,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47302,"sender_id":335,"sender_full_name":"bezem","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/976fe996b6de88bb8bc6aeecf4906d78?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47302\">#9</a> After what I read on http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Elephant (see <a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47297\">#6</a> above), I would be most happy with the following parenthesis added:  \"A geometric theory is a (possibly infinitary) first order theory ...\" (twice). An internal link to \"infinitary first order theory\" would be valuable (but there is no chapter on that). I'm not particularly fond of red herrings, but what I propose is actually a red herring: an infinitary first order theory is not a first order theory. The standard counterexample is \\bigvee_n \\phi_n, with \\phi_n the formula \\exists x_1,\\ldots,x_n.~\\forall y.~(y=x_1 \\lor \\cdots y=x_n). This infinite disjunction expresses finiteness, which is not first-order definable (because of compactness).</p>","timestamp":1403127836,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47303,"sender_id":138,"sender_full_name":"Zhen Lin","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/5284233bbaf108722eea4bbf89fdeda2?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47303\">#10</a> OK. I have edited the article, in part also to clarify that axioms are sequents rather than just formulae.</p>","timestamp":1403128415,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47304,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47304\">#11</a> Thanks to you two, for improving on the nLab entry!</p>\n<p>For bystanders: the edits are at <em><a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/geometic%20theory\">geometic theory</a></em> in the <a href=\"http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/diff/geometric+theory#idea\">Idea section</a>.</p>","timestamp":1403133831,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47313,"sender_id":335,"sender_full_name":"bezem","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/976fe996b6de88bb8bc6aeecf4906d78?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47313\">#12</a> Thanks to Zhen for the editing. Maybe a last remark: it seems that there is a typo in the example concerning torsion: n&gt;1 should be n&gt;0 to include the disjunct x=0 written as 1*x=0.</p>","timestamp":1403176340,"subject":"definition of geometric theory","stream_id":1,"display_recipient":"nLab","reactions":[]},{"id":47317,"sender_id":204,"sender_full_name":"IngoBlechschmidt","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/eb9f7e57a3c762c6d98139a2a349fae9?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/1-nLab/topic/definition.20of.20geometric.20theory/near/47317\">#13</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>4: Is the result still true for infinitary disjunctions? (I mean, pulling boxes to the front)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, it is. And it is false for infinitary conjuctions.</p>\n<p>In more detail, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mo>⋁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi></msup><mo>≡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><msub><mo>⋁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi></msubsup><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><msub><mo>⋁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\bigvee_i \\varphi_i)^\\Box \\equiv \\Box \\bigvee_i \\varphi_i^\\Box \\Rightarrow \\Box \\bigvee_i \\varphi_i</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1352em;vertical-align:-0.2997em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mop op-symbol small-op\" style=\"position:relative;top:0em;\">⋁</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.162em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4003em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2997em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">φ</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose\"><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8355em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord amsrm mtight\">□</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≡</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.1352em;vertical-align:-0.2997em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">□</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\"><span class=\"mop op-symbol small-op\" style=\"position:relative;top:0em;\">⋁</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.162em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4003em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.0